{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KqyNd71uAXcf1S9byBifC2","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→defmodule LiveVite.Slots do\n     2→  @moduledoc false\n     3→\n     4→  import Phoenix.Component\n     5→\n     6→  @doc false\n     7→  def rendered_slot_map(assigns) when assigns == %{}, do: %{}\n     8→\n     9→  def rendered_slot_map(assigns) do\n    10→    for(\n    11→      {key, [%{__slot__: _}] = slot} <- assigns,\n    12→      into: %{},\n    13→      do:\n    14→        case(key) do\n    15→          # we raise here because :inner_block is always there and we want to avoid\n    16→          # it overriding the default slot content\n    17→          :default -> raise \"Instead of using <:default> use <:inner_block> slot\"\n    18→          :inner_block -> {:default, render(%{slot: slot})}\n    19→          slot_name -> {slot_name, render(%{slot: slot})}\n    20→        end\n    21→    )\n    22→  end\n    23→\n    24→  @doc false\n    25→  def base_encode_64(assigns) do\n    26→    for {key, value} <- assigns, into: %{}, do: {key, Base.encode64(value)}\n    27→  end\n    28→\n    29→  @doc false\n    30→  defp render(assigns) do\n    31→    ~H\"\"\"\n    32→    <%= if assigns[:slot] do %>\n    33→      {render_slot(@slot)}\n    34→    <% end %>\n    35→    \"\"\"\n    36→    |> Phoenix.HTML.Safe.to_iodata()\n    37→    |> List.to_string()\n    38→    |> String.trim()\n    39→  end\n    40→end\n    41→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0186sJbD2wUdJtBvguBQXVMf","session_id":"f7614b6c-f448-43a7-8572-6bc7e0743cbe","uuid":"038aaf54-008d-4bb9-9042-2fee46669fd6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0186sJbD2wUdJtBvguBQXVMf","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the codebase. Let me create a detailed summary document.\n\n## Detailed Analysis: Vue Rendering Architecture in LiveVite\n\nBased on my thorough exploration of the LiveVite codebase, here's a comprehensive breakdown of how Vue rendering works:\n\n### 1. **TypeScript/JS Side: Vue App Mounting and Lifecycle**\n\n#### Hook Lifecycle (assets/hooks.ts)\n\nThe core integration happens through Phoenix LiveView hooks, which map to Vue lifecycle phases:\n\n- **`mounted()`**: Initializes the Vue app\n  - Retrieves component name from `data-name` attribute\n  - Calls `resolve()` function to load the component dynamically\n  - Chooses between `createApp()` (client) or `createSSRApp()` (server-rendered)\n  - Extracts props, slots, and handlers from HTML attributes\n  - Applies initial JSON patches for streams\n  - Calls user-provided `setup()` function (or uses defaultSetup)\n  - Stores app instance in `this.vue.app` for later updates\n\n- **`updated()`**: Handles prop changes after initial render\n  - Applies minimal JSON patches using `applyPatch()` for changed props (if diff mode enabled)\n  - Updates reactive props object in-place to trigger Vue reactivity\n  - Applies stream diffs (always sent)\n  - Updates slots\n\n- **`destroyed()`**: Cleans up the Vue app\n  - Waits for `phx:page-loading-stop` event before unmounting\n  - Calls `instance.unmount()` to destroy the Vue app\n\n#### Key Design: Reactive Props Object\n\nThe critical pattern is storing props in a **reactive object** (`reactive(getProps(...))`):\n```typescript\nconst props = reactive(getProps(this.el, this.liveSocket))\n// Updates are mutations that Vue automatically detects:\nObject.assign(this.vue.props, newProps)\napplyPatch(this.vue.props, diffs)\n```\n\nThis maintains Vue's reactivity system by modifying the existing reactive object rather than replacing it.\n\n#### Setup Function (assets/app.ts)\n\nThe default setup creates a Vue app and mounts it:\n```typescript\nconst app = createApp({ render: () => h(component, props, slots) })\napp.use(plugin)  // inject LiveVite instance\napp.mount(el)\nreturn app\n```\n\nUsers can override `setup` for custom configurations (e.g., adding plugins, configuring Pinia, etc.).\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Data Flow: Props and Slots**\n\n#### Props Parsing (assets/hooks.ts)\n- **`data-props`**: Full props object (sent on initial render or every update if diff disabled)\n- **`data-props-diff`**: JSON Patch operations for changed props only\n- **`data-handlers`**: Event handlers as JS operations, converted to `onEventName` props\n- **`data-streams-diff`**: Special patches for Phoenix LiveStream updates\n- Props are passed directly to Vue's `h()` render function, triggering reactivity\n\n#### Slots Parsing (assets/hooks.ts)\n- **`data-slots`**: Base64-encoded HTML content for each named slot\n- Converted to slot functions that render divs with `innerHTML`:\n```typescript\nconst getSlots = (el) => {\n  const dataSlots = getAttributeJson(el, \"data-slots\") || {}\n  return mapValues(dataSlots, base64 => () => \n    h(\"div\", { innerHTML: fromUtf8Base64(base64).trim() })\n  )\n}\n```\n- Slots are stored in reactive object, updated via `Object.assign()`\n\n---\n\n### 3. **JSON Patch Implementation (assets/jsonPatch.ts)**\n\nCustom JSON Patch operations for efficient updates:\n\n**Standard RFC 6902 operations:**\n- `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`\n\n**Custom operations for LiveView:**\n- **`upsert`**: Insert or update array items by `__dom_id` (for streams)\n- **`limit`**: Trim arrays to N elements (for stream limits)\n\n**Key features:**\n- In-place mutations maintain Vue reactivity\n- Special `$$id` syntax for array operations: `/items/$$dom-id-123` finds item by ID\n- Path component unescaping for JSON pointer syntax\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Elixir Side: Server Rendering and Props Encoding**\n\n#### Main Component Function (lib/live_vite.ex)\n\nThe `vue()` component function:\n\n1. **Determines render strategy**:\n   - Initial/dead render (`init or dead`) with SSR enabled → SSR\n   - Connected LiveView → client-side only\n\n2. **Extracts props by type**:\n   - `:props` - standard attributes\n   - `:handlers` - `v-on:*` attributes with JS operations\n   - `:slots` - Phoenix slot blocks\n   - `:streams` - Phoenix LiveStream objects\n\n3. **Calculates diffs**:\n   - `calculate_props_diff()`: Uses `__changed__` map to generate JSON Patch operations\n   - For simple types: `{op: \"replace\", path: \"/key\", value: ...}`\n   - For complex types: Uses `Jsonpatch.diff()` for minimal changes\n   - Special handling for streams with `upsert` and `limit` operations\n\n4. **Renders HTML**:\n```heex\n<div\n  id={id}\n  data-name={component_name}\n  data-props={json(props)}\n  data-props-diff={json(diffs)}\n  data-handlers={json(handlers)}\n  data-slots={base64_encode(slots)}\n  phx-hook=\"VueHook\"\n  phx-update=\"ignore\"\n>\n  <!-- SSR HTML here -->\n</div>\n```\n\n#### Props Encoding (lib/live_vite/encoder.ex)\n\nProtocol-based encoding to safely convert Elixir values to JSON:\n\n- **Primitive types**: Pass through as-is (Integer, Float, String, Atom)\n- **Collections**: Recursively encode lists and maps\n- **Date/Time**: Convert to ISO8601 strings\n- **Phoenix Forms**: Extract values, errors, and validity\n- **Ecto Changesets**: Collect values from changes and embedded associations\n- **Structs**: Require explicit protocol implementation via `@derive`\n\nThis prevents accidental exposure of private fields and ensures consistent encoding.\n\n#### Stream Handling (lib/live_vite.ex - generate_stream_patches)\n\nConverts Phoenix LiveStream operations to JSON Patch:\n- **Reset**: `{op: \"replace\", path: \"/stream_name\", value: []}`\n- **Deletes**: `{op: \"remove\", path: \"/stream_name/$$dom-id\"}`\n- **Inserts**: `{op: \"upsert\", path: \"/stream_name/0\", value: item}` with `__dom_id` added\n- **Limits**: `{op: \"limit\", path: \"/stream_name\", value: N}`\n\n---\n\n### 5. **SSR (Server-Side Rendering)**\n\n#### Vite Plugin (assets/vitePlugin.js)\n\nProvides HTTP endpoint `/ssr_render` for server-side rendering:\n\n```javascript\nconfigureServer(server) {\n  server.middlewares.use(async (req, res, next) => {\n    if (req.method === \"POST\" && req.url === \"/ssr_render\") {\n      const { name, props, slots } = req.body\n      const html = await render(name, props, slots)\n      res.end(html)\n    }\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe `render` function is loaded from `assets/server.ts`.\n\n#### Server.ts (assets/server.ts)\n\nSSR rendering implementation:\n\n1. **Component resolution**: Same `resolve()` function as client\n2. **Mock LiveView context**: Provides stub for composables (no real socket)\n3. **App creation**: Uses `createSSRApp` and custom setup\n4. **Rendering**: Vue's `renderToString(app, ctx)`\n5. **Preload generation**: Uses Vite manifest to generate `<link rel=\"modulepreload\">` tags\n\nReturns preload links + HTML as a single string.\n\n#### Two SSR Modes:\n\n- **NodeJS** (lib/live_vite/ssr/node_js.ex): Calls bundled server.mjs via NodeJS.Supervisor\n- **ViteJS** (lib/live_vite/ssr/vite_js.ex): POSTs to Vite dev server HTTP endpoint\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Composables and Integration (assets/use.ts)**\n\nProvides Vue composables for LiveView interaction:\n\n- **`useLiveVite()`**: Access the hook instance and props\n- **`useLiveEvent(name, callback)`**: Subscribe to server events\n- **`useLiveNavigation()`**: `patch()` and `navigate()` helpers\n- **`useLiveUpload(uploadConfig)`**: File upload with Phoenix integration\n- **`useEventReply(eventName)`**: Execute events with replies and loading state\n- **`useLiveConnection()`**: Monitor WebSocket connection state\n- **`useLiveForm(formName)`**: Form handling with Ecto changesets\n\nAll composables inject `_live_vite` key (set by the plugin).\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Framework Abstraction Opportunities**\n\nFor a React/Preact renderer interface, the following should be abstracted:\n\n**Core Plugin Interface:**\n```typescript\ninterface FrameworkRenderer {\n  // Resolve component by name\n  resolve(name: string): Promise<Component>\n  \n  // Setup function - create and mount app\n  setup(context: SetupContext): App\n  \n  // Hook into reactive system\n  makeReactive<T>(obj: T): T\n  \n  // Update mechanism\n  updateProps(obj: T, newProps: Partial<T>): void\n  updateSlots(obj: Record<string, Slot>, newSlots: Record<string, Slot>): void\n  \n  // Cleanup\n  destroy(app: App): void\n}\n```\n\n**Shared Infrastructure (Framework-agnostic):**\n- JSON Patch application logic (already generic in `jsonPatch.ts`)\n- Props encoding (Elixir side, protocol-based)\n- Slot HTML handling\n- Stream diff calculations\n- LiveView hook lifecycle management\n- Event handler translation\n- Composable/Hook registry\n\n**Framework-Specific:**\n- App creation (Vue: `createApp`, React: `ReactDOM.createRoot`)\n- Reactivity system (Vue: `reactive()`, React: `useState`)\n- Rendering function (Vue: `h()`, React: `createElement`)\n- Slot implementation (Vue: render functions, React: children/slots prop)\n- Cleanup timing and unmount behavior\n\n---\n\n### 8. **Key Data Structures**\n\n**Hook State (this.vue):**\n```typescript\n{\n  props: Record<string, any>    // reactive object\n  slots: Record<string, () => any>  // reactive slots\n  app: App<Element>             // framework app instance\n}\n```\n\n**Elixir Assigns for Rendering:**\n```elixir\n%{\n  __component_name: string,\n  props: Map,\n  props_diff: List,\n  streams_diff: List,\n  handlers: Map,\n  slots: Map,\n  use_diff: boolean,\n  ssr_render: %{preloadLinks: string, html: string} | nil\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThe architecture is remarkably clean and framework-agnostic at its core:\n\n1. **Elixir handles**: Component discovery, SSR, props encoding, diff calculation, HTML generation\n2. **JavaScript handles**: Hook lifecycle, prop updates via mutations, Vue app mounting\n3. **Framework-specific**: Only the app creation, reactivity system, and rendering logic\n4. **Shared utilities**: JSON Patch operations, event handling, composable system\n\nTo support React, you'd primarily need to:\n- Create a React `setup()` function using `ReactDOM.createRoot()` \n- Implement reactive props using hooks (`useState`)\n- Handle slot children via props\n- Manage cleanup in `destroyed()` lifecycle\n- Keep all Elixir-side logic identical\n\nThe JSON Patch system and LiveView integration are already completely framework-agnostic."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a3cfd38 (for resuming to continue this agent's work if needed)\n<usage>total_tokens: 53322\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 45860</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f7614b6c-f448-43a7-8572-6bc7e0743cbe","uuid":"d8a8515d-2a25-4137-9670-05323d8c3cff","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Explore the codebase at /home/andre/Documents/scratch/live_vue to understand how Vue rendering currently works. I need to understand:\n\n1. The TypeScript/JS side: How Vue components are mounted, updated, and destroyed in the LiveView hooks. Look at assets/hooks.ts, assets/index.ts, and any related files.\n2. The Vite plugin: How components are discovered and registered (assets/vitePlugin.js)\n3. The Elixir side: How components are rendered server-side (lib/live_vite/components.ex, lib/live_vite/ssr/)\n4. Any existing abstractions or patterns that could inform a renderer interface\n\nPlease read the full contents of these key files and provide a detailed summary of:\n- How Vue apps are created and mounted\n- How props are passed and updated\n- How SSR works\n- How the hook lifecycle (mounted, updated, destroyed) maps to Vue lifecycle\n- Any Vue-specific code that would need to be abstracted for other frameworks\n\nAlso check if there's a reference to a React/Astro project at /home/andre/Documents/scratch/astro that might show how a React renderer could look.","agentId":"a3cfd38","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the codebase. Let me create a detailed summary document.\n\n## Detailed Analysis: Vue Rendering Architecture in LiveVite\n\nBased on my thorough exploration of the LiveVite codebase, here's a comprehensive breakdown of how Vue rendering works:\n\n### 1. **TypeScript/JS Side: Vue App Mounting and Lifecycle**\n\n#### Hook Lifecycle (assets/hooks.ts)\n\nThe core integration happens through Phoenix LiveView hooks, which map to Vue lifecycle phases:\n\n- **`mounted()`**: Initializes the Vue app\n  - Retrieves component name from `data-name` attribute\n  - Calls `resolve()` function to load the component dynamically\n  - Chooses between `createApp()` (client) or `createSSRApp()` (server-rendered)\n  - Extracts props, slots, and handlers from HTML attributes\n  - Applies initial JSON patches for streams\n  - Calls user-provided `setup()` function (or uses defaultSetup)\n  - Stores app instance in `this.vue.app` for later updates\n\n- **`updated()`**: Handles prop changes after initial render\n  - Applies minimal JSON patches using `applyPatch()` for changed props (if diff mode enabled)\n  - Updates reactive props object in-place to trigger Vue reactivity\n  - Applies stream diffs (always sent)\n  - Updates slots\n\n- **`destroyed()`**: Cleans up the Vue app\n  - Waits for `phx:page-loading-stop` event before unmounting\n  - Calls `instance.unmount()` to destroy the Vue app\n\n#### Key Design: Reactive Props Object\n\nThe critical pattern is storing props in a **reactive object** (`reactive(getProps(...))`):\n```typescript\nconst props = reactive(getProps(this.el, this.liveSocket))\n// Updates are mutations that Vue automatically detects:\nObject.assign(this.vue.props, newProps)\napplyPatch(this.vue.props, diffs)\n```\n\nThis maintains Vue's reactivity system by modifying the existing reactive object rather than replacing it.\n\n#### Setup Function (assets/app.ts)\n\nThe default setup creates a Vue app and mounts it:\n```typescript\nconst app = createApp({ render: () => h(component, props, slots) })\napp.use(plugin)  // inject LiveVite instance\napp.mount(el)\nreturn app\n```\n\nUsers can override `setup` for custom configurations (e.g., adding plugins, configuring Pinia, etc.).\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Data Flow: Props and Slots**\n\n#### Props Parsing (assets/hooks.ts)\n- **`data-props`**: Full props object (sent on initial render or every update if diff disabled)\n- **`data-props-diff`**: JSON Patch operations for changed props only\n- **`data-handlers`**: Event handlers as JS operations, converted to `onEventName` props\n- **`data-streams-diff`**: Special patches for Phoenix LiveStream updates\n- Props are passed directly to Vue's `h()` render function, triggering reactivity\n\n#### Slots Parsing (assets/hooks.ts)\n- **`data-slots`**: Base64-encoded HTML content for each named slot\n- Converted to slot functions that render divs with `innerHTML`:\n```typescript\nconst getSlots = (el) => {\n  const dataSlots = getAttributeJson(el, \"data-slots\") || {}\n  return mapValues(dataSlots, base64 => () => \n    h(\"div\", { innerHTML: fromUtf8Base64(base64).trim() })\n  )\n}\n```\n- Slots are stored in reactive object, updated via `Object.assign()`\n\n---\n\n### 3. **JSON Patch Implementation (assets/jsonPatch.ts)**\n\nCustom JSON Patch operations for efficient updates:\n\n**Standard RFC 6902 operations:**\n- `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`\n\n**Custom operations for LiveView:**\n- **`upsert`**: Insert or update array items by `__dom_id` (for streams)\n- **`limit`**: Trim arrays to N elements (for stream limits)\n\n**Key features:**\n- In-place mutations maintain Vue reactivity\n- Special `$$id` syntax for array operations: `/items/$$dom-id-123` finds item by ID\n- Path component unescaping for JSON pointer syntax\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Elixir Side: Server Rendering and Props Encoding**\n\n#### Main Component Function (lib/live_vite.ex)\n\nThe `vue()` component function:\n\n1. **Determines render strategy**:\n   - Initial/dead render (`init or dead`) with SSR enabled → SSR\n   - Connected LiveView → client-side only\n\n2. **Extracts props by type**:\n   - `:props` - standard attributes\n   - `:handlers` - `v-on:*` attributes with JS operations\n   - `:slots` - Phoenix slot blocks\n   - `:streams` - Phoenix LiveStream objects\n\n3. **Calculates diffs**:\n   - `calculate_props_diff()`: Uses `__changed__` map to generate JSON Patch operations\n   - For simple types: `{op: \"replace\", path: \"/key\", value: ...}`\n   - For complex types: Uses `Jsonpatch.diff()` for minimal changes\n   - Special handling for streams with `upsert` and `limit` operations\n\n4. **Renders HTML**:\n```heex\n<div\n  id={id}\n  data-name={component_name}\n  data-props={json(props)}\n  data-props-diff={json(diffs)}\n  data-handlers={json(handlers)}\n  data-slots={base64_encode(slots)}\n  phx-hook=\"VueHook\"\n  phx-update=\"ignore\"\n>\n  <!-- SSR HTML here -->\n</div>\n```\n\n#### Props Encoding (lib/live_vite/encoder.ex)\n\nProtocol-based encoding to safely convert Elixir values to JSON:\n\n- **Primitive types**: Pass through as-is (Integer, Float, String, Atom)\n- **Collections**: Recursively encode lists and maps\n- **Date/Time**: Convert to ISO8601 strings\n- **Phoenix Forms**: Extract values, errors, and validity\n- **Ecto Changesets**: Collect values from changes and embedded associations\n- **Structs**: Require explicit protocol implementation via `@derive`\n\nThis prevents accidental exposure of private fields and ensures consistent encoding.\n\n#### Stream Handling (lib/live_vite.ex - generate_stream_patches)\n\nConverts Phoenix LiveStream operations to JSON Patch:\n- **Reset**: `{op: \"replace\", path: \"/stream_name\", value: []}`\n- **Deletes**: `{op: \"remove\", path: \"/stream_name/$$dom-id\"}`\n- **Inserts**: `{op: \"upsert\", path: \"/stream_name/0\", value: item}` with `__dom_id` added\n- **Limits**: `{op: \"limit\", path: \"/stream_name\", value: N}`\n\n---\n\n### 5. **SSR (Server-Side Rendering)**\n\n#### Vite Plugin (assets/vitePlugin.js)\n\nProvides HTTP endpoint `/ssr_render` for server-side rendering:\n\n```javascript\nconfigureServer(server) {\n  server.middlewares.use(async (req, res, next) => {\n    if (req.method === \"POST\" && req.url === \"/ssr_render\") {\n      const { name, props, slots } = req.body\n      const html = await render(name, props, slots)\n      res.end(html)\n    }\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe `render` function is loaded from `assets/server.ts`.\n\n#### Server.ts (assets/server.ts)\n\nSSR rendering implementation:\n\n1. **Component resolution**: Same `resolve()` function as client\n2. **Mock LiveView context**: Provides stub for composables (no real socket)\n3. **App creation**: Uses `createSSRApp` and custom setup\n4. **Rendering**: Vue's `renderToString(app, ctx)`\n5. **Preload generation**: Uses Vite manifest to generate `<link rel=\"modulepreload\">` tags\n\nReturns preload links + HTML as a single string.\n\n#### Two SSR Modes:\n\n- **NodeJS** (lib/live_vite/ssr/node_js.ex): Calls bundled server.mjs via NodeJS.Supervisor\n- **ViteJS** (lib/live_vite/ssr/vite_js.ex): POSTs to Vite dev server HTTP endpoint\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Composables and Integration (assets/use.ts)**\n\nProvides Vue composables for LiveView interaction:\n\n- **`useLiveVite()`**: Access the hook instance and props\n- **`useLiveEvent(name, callback)`**: Subscribe to server events\n- **`useLiveNavigation()`**: `patch()` and `navigate()` helpers\n- **`useLiveUpload(uploadConfig)`**: File upload with Phoenix integration\n- **`useEventReply(eventName)`**: Execute events with replies and loading state\n- **`useLiveConnection()`**: Monitor WebSocket connection state\n- **`useLiveForm(formName)`**: Form handling with Ecto changesets\n\nAll composables inject `_live_vite` key (set by the plugin).\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Framework Abstraction Opportunities**\n\nFor a React/Preact renderer interface, the following should be abstracted:\n\n**Core Plugin Interface:**\n```typescript\ninterface FrameworkRenderer {\n  // Resolve component by name\n  resolve(name: string): Promise<Component>\n  \n  // Setup function - create and mount app\n  setup(context: SetupContext): App\n  \n  // Hook into reactive system\n  makeReactive<T>(obj: T): T\n  \n  // Update mechanism\n  updateProps(obj: T, newProps: Partial<T>): void\n  updateSlots(obj: Record<string, Slot>, newSlots: Record<string, Slot>): void\n  \n  // Cleanup\n  destroy(app: App): void\n}\n```\n\n**Shared Infrastructure (Framework-agnostic):**\n- JSON Patch application logic (already generic in `jsonPatch.ts`)\n- Props encoding (Elixir side, protocol-based)\n- Slot HTML handling\n- Stream diff calculations\n- LiveView hook lifecycle management\n- Event handler translation\n- Composable/Hook registry\n\n**Framework-Specific:**\n- App creation (Vue: `createApp`, React: `ReactDOM.createRoot`)\n- Reactivity system (Vue: `reactive()`, React: `useState`)\n- Rendering function (Vue: `h()`, React: `createElement`)\n- Slot implementation (Vue: render functions, React: children/slots prop)\n- Cleanup timing and unmount behavior\n\n---\n\n### 8. **Key Data Structures**\n\n**Hook State (this.vue):**\n```typescript\n{\n  props: Record<string, any>    // reactive object\n  slots: Record<string, () => any>  // reactive slots\n  app: App<Element>             // framework app instance\n}\n```\n\n**Elixir Assigns for Rendering:**\n```elixir\n%{\n  __component_name: string,\n  props: Map,\n  props_diff: List,\n  streams_diff: List,\n  handlers: Map,\n  slots: Map,\n  use_diff: boolean,\n  ssr_render: %{preloadLinks: string, html: string} | nil\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThe architecture is remarkably clean and framework-agnostic at its core:\n\n1. **Elixir handles**: Component discovery, SSR, props encoding, diff calculation, HTML generation\n2. **JavaScript handles**: Hook lifecycle, prop updates via mutations, Vue app mounting\n3. **Framework-specific**: Only the app creation, reactivity system, and rendering logic\n4. **Shared utilities**: JSON Patch operations, event handling, composable system\n\nTo support React, you'd primarily need to:\n- Create a React `setup()` function using `ReactDOM.createRoot()` \n- Implement reactive props using hooks (`useState`)\n- Handle slot children via props\n- Manage cleanup in `destroyed()` lifecycle\n- Keep all Elixir-side logic identical\n\nThe JSON Patch system and LiveView integration are already completely framework-agnostic."}],"totalDurationMs":45860,"totalTokens":53322,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1138,"cache_read_input_tokens":49220,"output_tokens":2957,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1138,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0}}}}
